<h2>The mechanism in one view</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 1 — Supply and acceptance.</strong> Goods or services are supplied by a micro or small enterprise; the Section 15 clock runs from acceptance or deemed acceptance (no objection within 15 days).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 2 — The agreed window.</strong> Written agreement: up to 45 days, no more. No written agreement: 15 days (the "appointed day").</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 3 — Year-end test.</strong> Amounts paid within the window are deductible on accrual as usual. Amounts outstanding beyond the window at year-end are disallowed for that year.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Step 4 — Deduction on payment.</strong> The disallowed amount becomes deductible in the year it is actually paid — a timing disallowance, but with real cash-flow and tax-outgo consequences.</div>
</div>

<h2>What buyers are now doing — and should be</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Vendor classification files:</strong> obtaining Udyam registration numbers and declarations from every vendor, refreshed annually, because the disallowance turns on the supplier\'s micro/small status.</li>
  <li><strong>Ageing by statute, not habit:</strong> accounts-payable systems re-cut to flag the 15/45-day MSMED windows, not the commercial credit period the buyer would prefer.</li>
  <li><strong>Contract hygiene:</strong> written agreements fixing the permissible period — remembering that a clause beyond 45 days is ineffective for this purpose, since Section 15 caps the agreed period.</li>
  <li><strong>Quarter-four discipline:</strong> the balances that matter are those crossing the statutory window around year-end; March payment runs now routinely prioritise micro and small vendors.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>What suppliers should take from it</h2>
<p>The clause has changed negotiating dynamics in the supplier\'s favour in a way Section 16 interest never quite did, because the counterparty\'s own tax team now polices the deadline. A micro or small enterprise chasing payment should ensure the buyer\'s records can classify it correctly: quote the Udyam number on every invoice, state the MSMED status in the contract, and respond to vendor-classification requests promptly. In a dispute, the same documentation that preserves the buyer\'s deduction — acceptance dates, invoices, the written credit period — is the supplier\'s evidence before the Facilitation Council. And where a buyer seeks a certificate or confirmation that dues are not MSMED-covered, a supplier should understand exactly what it is being asked to sign away.</p>

<h2>Points of friction to watch</h2>
<p>Several recurring questions are working themselves out in assessments and advance planning: the treatment of amounts disputed in good faith (the MSMED interest provisions have their own logic for disputed sums, but clause (h) speaks of sums payable); the position of traders, who are brought into Udyam for limited benefits but whose coverage under the Chapter V definitions has its own contours; and year-end provisioning for unbilled supplies. These are fact-specific, and positions should be taken with contemporaneous documentation rather than assumed. The safe harbour, in every scenario, is the same: pay micro and small suppliers within the statutory window.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> This article describes the interface of two statutes in general terms and is not tax advice; assessments turn on individual facts, and positions on disputed or borderline items should be settled with the enterprise\'s tax advisors. On the recovery side, the MSMED machinery — demand, Section 18 reference, conciliation, arbitration — remains the supplier\'s operative remedy, and timelines there are unforgiving of delay.</p>
</div>
